[[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] as a [[Premier League]] team entered the [[2017–18 FA Cup|FA Cup]] in the third round, and were drawn against [[EFL Championship|Championship]] club [[Norwich City F.C.|Norwich City]] on 7 January at [[Carrow Road]]. Chelsea made nine changes to their starting eleven from their previous league match against [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] but still dominated the match, which ended goalless.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/42519706 |title=Norwich City 0–0 Chelsea |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=9 July 2020 |date=6 January 2018 |first=James |last=Law |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191107135011/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/42519706 |archive-date=7 November 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref> The replay was held 11 days later and was a 1–1 draw with goals from [[Michy Batshuayi]] in the 55th minute and [[Jamal Lewis (footballer)|Jamal Lewis]] in the 90th minute. The match went into [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]], during which Chelsea were reduced to nine men when both [[Pedro (footballer, born 1987)|Pedro]] and [[Álvaro Morata]] were dismissed, each for two [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow cards]]. With no further goals, the match went to a [[penalty shootout]] that Chelsea won 5–3 after their goalkeeper [[Willy Caballero]] saved [[Nélson Oliveira]]'s spot kick.<ref name=begley>{{cite web |last=Begley |first=Emlyn |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/42630626 |title=Chelsea 1–1 Norwich City (5–3 pens) |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=17 January 2018 |access-date=22 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180402021435/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/42630626 |archive-date=2 April 2018 |url-status=live }}</ref> The match was also notable for what the [[BBC]] reporter Emlyn Begley described as the "first video refereeing controversy" in English football when, after deliberation with [[Video assistant referee|VAR]], [[Willian (footballer, born 1988)|Willian]] was booked for diving when he was brought down by [[Timm Klose]] in the Norwich [[penalty area]]; [[Alan Shearer]] described the decision as a "shambles".<ref name=begley/> In the fourth round, Chelsea met Premier League opposition [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]], and won 3–0 with two goals from Batshuayi and one from [[Marcos Alonso (footballer, born 1990)|Marcos Alonso]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Mullen |first=Andrew |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/42763630 |title=Chelsea 3–0 Newcastle United |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=28 January 2018 |access-date=22 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180406033102/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/42763630 |archive-date=6 April 2018 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

In the fifth round, Chelsea faced [[Hull City A.F.C.|Hull City]], another Championship team, at home. Chelsea were dominant, scoring four times in the first half with goals from Willian (2), Pedro and [[Olivier Giroud]], winning the game 4–0.<ref name="Begley 2018">{{cite web |last=Begley |first=Emlyn |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/42998720 |title=Chelsea 4–0 Hull City |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=16 February 2018 |access-date=22 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180305074241/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/42998720 |archive-date=5 March 2018 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the quarter-finals, they visited the [[King Power Stadium]] to face fellow Premier League opponents [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]], where a goal for Chelsea from Morata and a Leicester equaliser from [[Jamie Vardy]] took the game to extra time. [[Substitute (association football)|Substitute]] Pedro came off the bench and scored the winning goal to take Chelsea to the semi-final.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/43361317 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=9 July 2020 |date=18 March 2018 |first=Phil |last=McNulty |author-link=Phil McNulty |title=Leicester 1–2 Chelsea |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190803060752/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/43361317 |archive-date=3 August 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref> There, they played Premier League opposition in [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] at Wembley, and goals from [[Forward (association football)|strikers]] Giroud and Morata saw Chelsea win 2–0, and progress to the FA Cup Final for the second successive season.<ref>{{cite web |last=Abraham |first=Timothy |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/43772431 |title=Chelsea 2–0 Southampton |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=22 April 2018 |access-date=18 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180508153353/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/43772431 |archive-date=8 May 2018 |url-status=live }}</ref>

